      "outputTokens": 799,
      "costUsd": 0.007248,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended with these thousand simulated bags of chips. The machine aims for one hundred and seventy-five grams. Most bags land close to that, a few land further away, and the two sides look like mirror images. Watch a few more bags fill. Each one gets about ninety chips, and each chip is a little heavier or lighter than the last. Heights, measurement errors and many other natural measurements often make a similar bell shape. So why does it keep turning up? When can we use it as a model? And what does its shape tell us?"
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-02",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-s09/scene-02.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 1661,
      "worstWordGap": {
