      "successfulRequestCount": 1,
      "loggedRequestAttempts": 1,
      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 196,
      "outputTokens": 1384,
      "costUsd": 0.012554,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's one reason. A bag's mass isn't set by one thing. Lots of small effects push it a little up or a little down, like one more chip, a bigger chip, or a few crumbs. Watch dots fall through twelve rows of pegs. Each peg nudges a dot left or right, with equal chance. To land at the far left, a dot has to go left all twelve times, and only one path does that. To land one bin in from the edge, it needs just one right nudge, and that nudge can come at any of the twelve rows, so twelve paths do. To land in the middle, it needs six of each, in any order, and nine hundred and twenty-four paths do that. So the dots pile up in the middle and thin out towards the edges, the same on both sides. When many small, independent effects add up, the total often has a shape close to a bell."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-03",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-s09/scene-03.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 1072,
