      "inputTokens": 137,
      "outputTokens": 964,
      "costUsd": 0.0087445,
      "pronunciationText": "So when is a normal model useful? Look for continuous data that bunch around one peak and thin out evenly in both directions. Then lay the bell over the data and check that the tails match too. These are clues, not a proof. Many small, independent effects adding up is a good sign. Household water use isn't like that. Most homes use a moderate amount, but a few use a lot, so the tail stretches far to the right. The bus wait from two videos ago is flat, not a bell. And a component's lifetime piles up near zero, then trails off. For those, a normal model would put the probability in the wrong places."
    },
